  <div class="story">
    <div><h3>The problem</h3><p>A creator who wants a sponsorship usually sends a brand a folder of screenshots, one from each platform's own dashboard. The numbers are stale the day they are sent, and the brand has no way to check them.</p><p>Talent managers repeat that work for every creator they represent, usually in a spreadsheet they update by hand.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What I built</h3><p>A site where every tracked creator already has a profile, pre-filled from public data. The creator claims it by signing in with each platform, which marks the profile as verified and keeps it refreshed without any manual work. The result is one link that stays current.</p><p>Managers get a roster dashboard with PDF and CSV export. The public side has game and category pages, trending rankings and paid industry reports.</p></div>
    <div><h3>What made it hard</h3><p>Twitch gives one app about 800 API points a minute, and page views, scheduled jobs and background sweeps all draw from that single allowance. I gave every call a priority that travels with it through async code, so a visitor's page never waits behind a nightly sweep.</p><p>The hosting platform caps a job step at 300 seconds. The heavy jobs moved to GitHub Actions workers, and the largest tier of creators is split into shards.</p></div>
  </div>

