  <div class="block-title"><h3>Under the floor</h3><span class="label">How it works</span></div>
  <div class="eng">
    <div class="eng-diagram" data-arch></div>
    <div class="eng-notes">
      <div><h4>Priority travels with the call</h4><p>Page requests and scheduled jobs run in the same Node process, so a per-process setting could not tell them apart. Each call is wrapped in an AsyncLocalStorage scope that names its priority, and everything it awaits inherits it. A page request has no scope: it never sleeps and fails fast if the bucket is empty.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Reserves instead of queues</h4><p>Each priority must leave a fixed number of points untouched: 50 for game jobs, 100 for ordinary jobs, 150 for tier snapshots, 350 for sweeps. A job that would dip below its reserve waits for the reset time Twitch reports in its response headers, then carries on. Low-priority work backs off first without any central queue.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Three tiers, two runtimes</h4><p>Creators over 100K followers are re-measured every six hours. The 10K to 100K tier runs daily at 02:00 UTC and everyone smaller at 03:00. Light jobs stay on Inngest. Anything that cannot finish inside the 300 second step cap runs on a GitHub Actions worker, and the largest tier is split into two shards.</p></div>
      <div><h4>Public pages cost almost nothing to serve</h4><p>Profiles, game pages and the landing page are rendered on the server and cached, with the landing page rebuilt every 600 seconds. The header stays static and loads the signed-in state separately, so one logged-in visitor does not force every page to render per request. OAuth tokens are stored encrypted with AES-256-GCM.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

